<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    nexus-tk070.p6b (RDR-194 § D5, § P6b) — nexus.frecency.ttl_days /
    staging.frecency.ttl_days: 0=permanent RETIRED, NULL=permanent adopted.
    Sibling of memory-003-ttl-days.xml / plans-003-ttl-days.xml (same bead
    family, RDR-194 D5) — the THIRD and LAST TTL column this Decision
    touches, and the only one that is a genuine REWRITE of a large
    population rather than a rename: frecency.ttl_days was already named
    correctly and already the column the CHECK belongs on; what changes
    here is the SENTINEL VALUE, not the column identity.

    WHY THIS COLUMN IS DIFFERENT FROM memory/plans. frecency.ttl_days
    (`INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0`, telemetry-001-baseline.xml:282) is the
    column the OTHER two TTL columns' fix intentionally did NOT converge
    onto (see memory-003-ttl-days.xml's header, "Why NULL and not
    frecency's 0"): 0=permanent is a legal integer TTL AND a sentinel at
    the same time, so no CHECK can defend it. `NULL` = permanent, already
    the contract for memory.ttl_days and plans.ttl_days (RDR-194 D5), is
    the ONLY semantics a CHECK can enforce — `CHECK (ttl_days IS NULL OR
    ttl_days > 0)`, identical shape to `memory_ttl_days_positive_chk` /
    `plans_ttl_days_positive_chk`.

    GATE PRECONDITION (D0.4): nexus-tk070.cc6 (cloud-count-6, T2-recorded,
    measured 2026-08-20T05:50:29Z) — nexus.frecency 0 zero-rows / 0 total
    rows; staging.frecency 0 zero-rows / 0 total rows. This migration is a
    ZERO-ROW no-op on the live store today. It ships fully guarded
    (counted UPDATE, RAISE NOTICE, RLS toggle, CHECK, ANALYZE) regardless
    of that measured zero — the count is evidence of what THIS box holds
    at migration time, never a structurally-guaranteed zero (identical
    reasoning to fk-004-0-reconcile-precount's / legacy-001's / memory-003's
    / plans-003's own headers). The TOTAL row count (not just the zero-row
    count) was gated first specifically because this is "the only large
    rewrite in the arc" (RDR-194 D5) and prices the mandatory ANALYZE
    (D0.5) — on this box, both counts are zero, so the ANALYZE is cheap,
    but the changeset makes no assumption about that.

    COUNTED UPDATE (not DELETE), RAISE NOTICE'd (D0.4 / migration-002-
    tenant-pk.xml and fk-004-chunks-collection-registry.xml's
    fk-004-0-reconcile-precount precedent, same counted-mutation shape as
    memory-003-1/plans-003-1 but UPDATE-not-DELETE since frecency's ttl=0
    rows are not unrepresentable data to discard — they are exactly the
    rows this migration RECOVERS by converting to the correct sentinel).

    RLS TOGGLE on the UPDATE. nexus.frecency carries FORCE ROW LEVEL
    SECURITY (telemetry-001-baseline.xml, changeset telemetry-001-12).
    Liquibase runs as nexus_admin, the table owner but NOT BYPASSRLS — an
    untoggled UPDATE under FORCE RLS with no nexus.tenant GUC set at
    migration time would silently update ZERO rows regardless of actual
    content (the tenant_isolation policy's USING/WITH CHECK predicate never
    matches a NULL GUC) — the identical silent-vacuity trap
    migration-002-tenant-pk.xml's, fk-004-0-reconcile-precount's, and
    memory-003-ttl-days.xml's headers document for their own reads/
    mutations. For an UPDATE this is doubly dangerous: not only would the
    RAISE NOTICE always report 0 by construction, the rows that SHOULD have
    converted would be silently left at the now-unrepresentable ttl_days=0
    value one changeset away from a CHECK that forbids it — exactly the
    kind of migration ordering hazard D0.3 exists to prevent. Toggled NO
    FORCE for the UPDATE, restored to FORCE immediately after (minimal
    window, same shape as fk-004-1-reconcile / memory-003-1 / plans-003-1).

    staging.frecency mirrors mechanically (RDR-194 D5: "staging.frecency
    follows nexus.frecency mechanically, as the staging mirror always
    does; staging remains otherwise exempt"). staging.frecency
    (staging-001-landing-tables.xml, changeset staging-3-pointer-stores)
    carries the IDENTICAL `ttl_days INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0` shape and
    the IDENTICAL FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY + tenant_isolation policy, so
    changeset -2 below mirrors -1's DROP DEFAULT / DROP NOT NULL / counted
    UPDATE / RLS-toggle / ANALYZE exactly. "Staging remains otherwise
    exempt" means the CHECK: staging is typeless landing by design
    (StagingPromoteOps' own class javadoc, quoted verbatim in
    legacy-001-drop-chash-alias.xml's header — "staging stays typeless...
    a malformed staged value fails LOUD at PROMOTE time, not at land
    time") — a CHECK there would reject legitimate arbitrary pre-
    validation ETL input; the promote path (StagingPromoteOps.java,
    ~1035-1070) is what canonicalizes or rejects. A staged row whose
    ttl_days violates the new CHECK still fails loud, just at PROMOTE
    time (the CHECK on nexus.frecency itself), matching legacy-001-3's
    identical precedent of adding the format CHECK to nexus.* but not to
    staging.*.

    ADD CHECK on nexus.frecency ONLY — `frecency_ttl_days_positive_chk`,
    named to match the `%_ttl_days_positive_chk` suffix
    HttpUtil.ttlDaysCheckRemedy already wildcard-matches (no engine change
    needed there — the remedy text fires automatically for this
    constraint the first time it is ever violated).

    ANALYZE, MANDATORY (D0.5 / tests/test_changelog_analyze_lint.py's
    documented rule, BUG-0148 precedent) — both tables, since both are
    UPDATE-rewritten by this file, matching legacy-001-7's "ANALYZE
    nexus.frecency, nexus.relevance_log" precedent of ANALYZE-ing every
    table a sibling changeset in the SAME file mutated.

    D0.6 ROLLBACK: restores the baseline shape exactly on both tables —
    drop the CHECK (nexus only), restore NOT NULL DEFAULT 0. Restoring
    NOT NULL DEFAULT 0 on a column that may hold NULL rows (either from
    this migration's own conversion, since NOTHING un-NULLs a
    permanent row on rollback by design, or from a NULL row written
    after this migration by any client honoring the new contract)
    requires REWRITING those NULLs back to 0 FIRST — rollback data-
    fidelity, the nexus-cck6z precedent already established by this
    arc's rollback-integrity work: a rollback that silently leaves rows
    NULL under a column it is about to mark NOT NULL does not merely
    lose data, it makes the ROLLBACK ITSELF fail with a NOT NULL
    violation the instant it tries to apply the constraint. Both
    directions are stated inline, not silently omitted.

    D0.1 placement: changelog TAIL, immediately after plans-003-ttl-
    days.xml (the current tail, this bead's own predecessor nexus-tk070.p6a)
    and before the grants-nexus-svc.xml runAlways block, matching the
    established late-append convention. EXPECT_NEW_CHANGESETS=2 for this
    bead's one file, two changesets (telemetry-006-1 for nexus.frecency,
    telemetry-006-2 for staging.frecency).

    CLIENT PAIRING: this migration ships with the T3 read-path predicate
    flip (src/nexus/db/http_vector_client.py's HttpVectorClient.expire(),
    src/nexus/db/t3.py's docstrings) in the SAME commit — see RDR-194 D5's
    "Wire contract change, stated per column" section and
    memory-003-ttl-days.xml's header for the full two-commit
    docs/wire-contract-pending.md ## Unshipped declaration mechanism this
    arc already established (the entry is added in the immediately-
    following commit, once this commit's own sha is known).
-->
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.4.xsd">

    <!-- ── Changeset telemetry-006-1: nexus.frecency — DROP DEFAULT/NOT NULL,
         counted UPDATE 0 -> NULL, ADD CHECK, ANALYZE ─────────────────────── -->
    <changeSet id="telemetry-006-1" author="nexus-tk070.p6b">
        <comment>
            nexus.frecency.ttl_days: 0=permanent RETIRED, NULL=permanent
            adopted. DROP DEFAULT + DROP NOT NULL, counted UPDATE
            ttl_days=NULL WHERE ttl_days=0 (RAISE NOTICE'd, RLS
            toggle-wrapped so FORCE RLS cannot hide rows from the count),
            ADD CHECK (ttl_days IS NULL OR ttl_days > 0), ANALYZE. Gated on
            nexus-tk070.cc6: 0 zero-rows / 0 total rows measured on the
            live store — this changeset is a no-op there today, but ships
            unconditionally guarded regardless.
        </comment>
        <sql splitStatements="false" stripComments="false">
ALTER TABLE nexus.frecency ALTER COLUMN ttl_days DROP DEFAULT;
ALTER TABLE nexus.frecency ALTER COLUMN ttl_days DROP NOT NULL;

ALTER TABLE nexus.frecency NO FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;

DO $$
DECLARE
    v_converted_count BIGINT;
BEGIN
    UPDATE nexus.frecency SET ttl_days = NULL WHERE ttl_days = 0;
    GET DIAGNOSTICS v_converted_count = ROW_COUNT;
    RAISE NOTICE 'nexus-tk070.p6b: converted % nexus.frecency row(s) from ttl_days = 0 to ttl_days = NULL — 0 is retired as the permanent sentinel (RDR-194 D5); NULL is the sole permanent sentinel going forward, enforced by the CHECK this changeset adds', v_converted_count;
END $$;

ALTER TABLE nexus.frecency FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;

ALTER TABLE nexus.frecency
    ADD CONSTRAINT frecency_ttl_days_positive_chk CHECK (ttl_days IS NULL OR ttl_days > 0);

ANALYZE nexus.frecency;
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <!-- D0.6: restores the baseline shape exactly. Rewrites any
                 NULL ttl_days rows back to 0 FIRST (nexus-cck6z rollback
                 data-fidelity precedent) — SET NOT NULL below would
                 otherwise fail outright against a NULL row left by either
                 this changeset's own conversion or any later NULL write a
                 client made under the new contract. This is a best-effort
                 schema restoration, not a claim that the ORIGINAL 0-tagged
                 identity of a since-converted row is recoverable (D0.6):
                 a row this changeset converted from 0 and a row some
                 client later wrote as NULL directly are indistinguishable
                 by the time rollback runs, so both land back at 0. -->
            <sql splitStatements="false" stripComments="false">
ALTER TABLE nexus.frecency DROP CONSTRAINT IF EXISTS frecency_ttl_days_positive_chk;

ALTER TABLE nexus.frecency NO FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
UPDATE nexus.frecency SET ttl_days = 0 WHERE ttl_days IS NULL;
ALTER TABLE nexus.frecency FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;

ALTER TABLE nexus.frecency ALTER COLUMN ttl_days SET NOT NULL;
ALTER TABLE nexus.frecency ALTER COLUMN ttl_days SET DEFAULT 0;
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <!-- ── Changeset telemetry-006-2: staging.frecency — DROP DEFAULT/NOT
         NULL, counted UPDATE 0 -> NULL, ANALYZE (NO CHECK — staging stays
         typeless landing) ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <changeSet id="telemetry-006-2" author="nexus-tk070.p6b">
        <comment>
            staging.frecency.ttl_days: mirrors telemetry-006-1 mechanically
            (RDR-194 D5) — DROP DEFAULT + DROP NOT NULL, counted UPDATE
            0 -> NULL (RAISE NOTICE'd, RLS toggle-wrapped), ANALYZE. NO
            CHECK constraint here: staging is typeless landing by design
            (StagingPromoteOps' own class javadoc), so a malformed staged
            ttl_days still fails loud at PROMOTE time against
            nexus.frecency's CHECK, not at land time — the identical
            staging-exemption precedent legacy-001-3 already established
            for the chunk_id format CHECK.
        </comment>
        <sql splitStatements="false" stripComments="false">
ALTER TABLE staging.frecency ALTER COLUMN ttl_days DROP DEFAULT;
ALTER TABLE staging.frecency ALTER COLUMN ttl_days DROP NOT NULL;

ALTER TABLE staging.frecency NO FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;

DO $$
DECLARE
    v_converted_count BIGINT;
BEGIN
    UPDATE staging.frecency SET ttl_days = NULL WHERE ttl_days = 0;
    GET DIAGNOSTICS v_converted_count = ROW_COUNT;
    RAISE NOTICE 'nexus-tk070.p6b: converted % staging.frecency row(s) from ttl_days = 0 to ttl_days = NULL — mirrors telemetry-006-1, no CHECK added (staging stays typeless landing)', v_converted_count;
END $$;

ALTER TABLE staging.frecency FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;

ANALYZE staging.frecency;
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <!-- D0.6, mirrors telemetry-006-1's rollback (no CHECK to drop
                 here — staging never had one). -->
            <sql splitStatements="false" stripComments="false">
ALTER TABLE staging.frecency NO FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
UPDATE staging.frecency SET ttl_days = 0 WHERE ttl_days IS NULL;
ALTER TABLE staging.frecency FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;

ALTER TABLE staging.frecency ALTER COLUMN ttl_days SET NOT NULL;
ALTER TABLE staging.frecency ALTER COLUMN ttl_days SET DEFAULT 0;
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
